<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sadr</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sadr was a title of high office in the Safavid state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In Safavid practice the sadr was closely associated with supervising the religious establishment and pious endowments, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> also mention the title alongside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a> in describing the senior official who managed civil affairs. The office reflected the close connection between state administration and Shia religious authority in Safavid Iran.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shah governed through a chief minister, the <b>sadr</b>-e a’zam, and through royal princes and other governors sent to the provinces, who collected taxes, often through tax farmers, and kept much of the revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…affairs were directed by the grand vizier (later styled etemad al-dowleh), the religious establishment was supervised by the <b>sadr</b>, and the qurchi-bashi commanded the Qizilbash royal guard; under Abbas I the ghulam system added a further power centre of slave-officials dependent on the Shah, deliberately counterbalancing the Qizilbash.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sadr</b> finds no evidence of widespread Khoekhoe settlements or large herds south of the Orange River before 1000 CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A minister, the <b>sadr</b>, managed the madad-i-ma’ash, tax-free grants of land revenue to the learned and the pious, and Jahangir gave many more than Akbar.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that the Sufi Shaikh Sadruddin <b>Sadr</b>-i-Jahan, of Sherwani Afghan descent, settled in the area in 1454 and married a daughter of Bahlul Lodi, receiving land grants.</blockquote><a href="/state/malerkotla-state">Malerkotla State</a></li></ul></section>
